Regarding error handling, whether to throw an error or quietly handle non-string inputs depends on the context in which the function will be used. If the function is part of a critical system where type safety is paramount, throwing an error might be the better choice to ensure that any type mismatches are immediately visible and addressed. On the other hand, if the function is part of a user-facing application where robustness and graceful error handling are key, quietly handling non-string inputs (for example, by returning false) might provide a better user experience, as it avoids presenting the user with technical error messages. Ultimately, the choice between these approaches should be guided by the specific requirements and constraints of the application.

